{
  "sn35.247:0.1": "Linked Discourses 35.247 ",
  "sn35.247:0.2": "19. The Simile of the Vipers ",
  "sn35.247:0.3": "The Simile of Six Animals ",
  "sn35.247:1.1": "“Mendicants, suppose a person with wounded and festering limbs was to enter a thicket of reeds. ",
  "sn35.247:1.2": "The kusa thorns would pierce their feet, and the reed leaves would scratch their limbs. ",
  "sn35.247:1.3": "And that would cause that person to experience even more pain and distress. ",
  "sn35.247:1.4": "In the same way, some mendicant goes to a village or a wilderness and gets scolded, ",
  "sn35.247:1.5": "‘This venerable, acting like this, behaving like this, is a filthy village thorn.’ ",
  "sn35.247:1.6": "Understanding that they’re a thorn, they should understand restraint and lack of restraint. ",
  "sn35.247:2.1": "And how is someone unrestrained? ",
  "sn35.247:2.2": "Take a mendicant who sees a sight with their eyes. If it’s pleasant they hold on to it, but if it’s unpleasant they dislike it. They live with mindfulness of the body unestablished and their heart stunted. ",
  "sn35.247:2.3": "And they don’t truly understand the freedom of heart and freedom by wisdom where those arisen bad, unskillful qualities cease without remainder. ",
  "sn35.247:2.4": "When they hear a sound with their ears … ",
  "sn35.247:2.5": "When they smell an odor with their nose … ",
  "sn35.247:2.6": "When they taste a flavor with their tongue … ",
  "sn35.247:2.7": "When they feel a touch with their body … ",
  "sn35.